<body>
    <div class="header">
        <div class="nasa-logo"></div>
        <div class="title">Global Significance of Serpentine on Mars</div>
        <div class="subtitle">Postdoc Research</div>
        <div class="author">
            Author: A. Emran (3223 JPL Postdoctoral Fellow)<br>
            J. D. Tarnas (Blue); and K. M. Stack (3223)
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="columns">
        <!-- Left Column -->
        <div class="col-left">
            <div class="section">
                <h2>INTRODUCTION</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Serpentine is an astrobiologically compelling mineral on Mars. H<sub>2</sub> and CH<sub>4</sub> (in presence of CO<sub>2</sub>) are produced during its production via the oxidation of olivine and pyroxene (mafic and ultra-mafic rocks) by water [1]
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Serpentine has characteristic absorption bands at 1.38, 2.12, 2.32, and 2.52 µm in near infrared wavelengths [3]
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; 2.12 µm is the most characteristic band for a reliable identification (Fig. 1)
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Identifying serpentine on Mars using I/F data from the Compact Reconnaissance Imaging Spectrometer for Mars (CRISM) is challenging due to the presence of an artifact in the images [4]
                    </p>
                    <p style="font-size:11px; font-style:italic;">Requires a validation of 2.12 µm band in radiance data – called radiance ratio technique [4] (Fig. 2)</p>
                </div>

                <div class="figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: Laboratory spectra of serpentine, dolomite, and Mg-rich chlorite (RELAB database) [5]]</div>
                    Fig. 1: Laboratory spectra of serpentine, dolomite, and Mg-rich chlorite (RELAB database) [5].
                </div>

                <div class="figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: Example of a false positive serpentine detection on Mars using CRISM data. (a) Location of serpentine claimed pixels. (b) Spectra showing the presence of 2.12 µm feature in the I/F data but absent in the radiance spectrum [6]]</div>
                    Fig. 2: Example of a false positive serpentine detection on Mars using CRISM data. (a) Location of serpentine claimed pixels. (b) Spectra showing the presence of 2.12 µm feature in the I/F data but absent in the radiance spectrum [6].
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="section">
                <h2>OBJECTIVE</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>&#10003; Create an updated global map of serpentine for Mars using CRISM images</p>
                    <p>&#10003; validated with both I/F and radiance data</p>
                    <p>&#10003; Geologic context and mineral assemblage associated with serpentine</p>
                    <p>&#10003; Timing and amount (approx.) of H<sub>2</sub> produced via serpentinization on Mars</p>
                    <p>&#10003; Extend of serpentinization on the surface and subsurface</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="section">
                <h2>METHODOLOGY</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>
                        &#10003; A total of 15,760 CRISM images (Reduced Data Record) from all geologic units
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Uses all TRDR records viz. 7885 Full Resolution Targeted (FRT), 2785 Half Resolution Long (HRL), 3738 Full Resolution Short (FRS), 1352 and Half Resolution Short (HRS)
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Covers 90° N to 90° S latitudes (~1.2% of Martian surface)
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Atmospherically and photometrically corrected using the standard CRISM processing pipeline including volcano-scan calibration [7]
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Apply dynamic aperture factor analysis/target transformation (DAFA/TT) [6] to the calibrated CRISM images
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; I/F and radiance ratio spectra from the DAFA/TT detected pixels
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Detections are assigned to confidence values – 1 (low/weak) to 5 (high/best)
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Geologic context using CTX and THEMIS-IR daytime mosaics [8-9]
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Age of serpentine host unit [10] and associated minerals from CRISM
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Influence of dust in surface detection using dust cover index map [11]
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        &#10003; Amount of H<sub>2</sub> produced using the rates from analogue rocks [12]
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Right Column -->
        <div class="col-right">
            <div class="section">
                <h2>RESULTS</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <div class="key-findings">
                        <strong># Key findings #</strong><br><br>
                        &#10003; Detected in 43 CRISM images, with detections in 32 of the images representing "new" or previously unrecognized detections<br>
                        &#10003; Occurrence between 40°N to 40°S latitudes, and no detections between 90°E to 180°E longitudes<br>
                        &#10003; Formation in a wide variety of landforms (craters, valleys, and plains)<br>
                        &#10003; 31 out of 43 detected images on Noachian-aged surface units<br>
                        &#10003; Younger detections are associated with impact craters – possibility that serpentine formed earlier at depth, and was subsequently excavated<br>
                        &#10003; Serpentine detections correspond with lower average dust cover<br>
                        &#10003; Mg-rich serpentine detections cover a total surface area of ~1.46 km²<br>
                        &#10003; Estimated max. production (~10 km) of 5.11 × 10¹¹ and 4.85 × 10³³ mol H₂
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: Global distribution of serpentine on Mars overlaid on the TES dust cover map on MOLA global map [12]. Serpentine detections are color-coded according to confidence level. Upper left inset shows number of images investigated in 30° longitudinal bin.]</div>
                    Fig. 3: Global distribution of serpentine on Mars overlaid on the TES dust cover map on MOLA global map [12]. Serpentine detections are color-coded according to confidence level. Upper left inset shows number of images investigated in 30° longitudinal bin.
                </div>

                <div class="results-grid">
                    <!-- Sub-figure A -->
                    <div>
                        <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: (a) Black outline represents HR0000S60c on CTX image. Upper left inset shows the regional context. (b) and (c) show the local geologic context (red polygon on CTX, (d) the average I/F (blue) and radiance (orange) ratio spectra of both pixel clusters.]</div>
                        <div style="text-align:center; font-size:10px;">Fig. 4: a) Black outline represents HR0000S60c on CTX image. Upper left inset shows the regional context. (b) and (c) show the local geologic context (red polygon on CTX, (d) the average I/F (blue) and radiance (orange) ratio spectra of both pixel clusters.</div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Sub-figure B and C -->
                    <div class="results-grid">
                        <div>
                            <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: (a) Average TES dust index values (right axis). (b) Longitudinal distribution of average TES dust index values and the number of CRISM images containing serpentine. Higher TES dust index values correspond to less pixel clusters.]</div>
                            <div style="text-align:center; font-size:10px;">Fig. 5: Characteristics of serpentine deposits on Mars. (a) Geologic setting of serpentine deposits. (b) Number of images containing serpentine and all images investigated, binned by the age of units they occur, plotted with the average TES dust index values (right axis). (c) Longitudinal distribution of average TES dust index values and the number of CRISM images containing serpentine. Higher TES dust index values correspond to less pixel clusters.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: (a) Total number of CRISM images containing serpentine. (b) Number of images containing serpentine and all images investigated, binned by the age of units they occur, plotted with the average TES dust index values (right axis). (c) Longitudinal distribution of average TES dust index values and the number of CRISM images containing serpentine. Higher TES dust index values correspond to less pixel clusters.]</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="section">
                <h2>DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <div class="key-findings">
                        <strong>Reasons for the apparent paucity of serpentine on Mars could include:</strong><br>
                        a) Limitations of orbiter sensor capability and the impact of dust and/or sub-pixel mixing<br>
                        b) Inability to uniquely detect Fe-rich serpentine in [16]<br>
                        c) CRISM data covers only ~1.2% of the Martian surface<br>
                        d) Serpentine may occur extensively on early Mars, but has subsequently weathered and altered to clay minerals and carbonates<br>
                        e) Serpentinization occurred primarily in the subsurface and is observable only when excavated by impact cratering or other tectonic processes
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">[Figure: Global distribution of serpentine on Mars overlaid on the TES dust cover map on MOLA global map [12]. Serpentine detections are color-coded according to confidence level. Upper left inset shows number of images investigated in 30° longitudinal bin.]</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

